{:edb/db-before-t 1779 :edb/db-after-t 1780 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535092 50 #inst "2026-10-08T16:40:37.980Z" 13194139535092 true] [17592186166494 1100 "Let $F(x) = \\cos(2x)$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R$. Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $[MATH: x \\in [0,\\pi)]$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x+\\pi) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n2. $f(x) = F(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n3. $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$" 13194139535092 false] [17592186166494 1100 "Let $F(x) = \\cos(2x)$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x \\in [0,\\pi)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f(x) = F(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\n- $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = f(x)$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$" 13194139535092 true] [17592186166494 1145 "Since $f$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$, we have\n\n- $f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, and\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $[MATH: x\\in[0,\\pi).]$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi$-periodicity:\n$f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$\nfor all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi$-periodic, because for any $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$, we have\n$F(x + π) = \\cos (2(x + π)) = \\cos (2x + 2π) = \\cos (2x) = F(x)$.\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0$. Then,\n\n$$\nf(x + \\frac{π}{2}) = f(\\frac{π}{2}) = F(\\frac{π}{2}) = \\cos (π) =-1\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\nf(x) = f(0) = F(0)\\cos (0) = 1\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nf\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\neq f(x)\n$$\n\nfor $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535092 false] [17592186166494 1145 "Since $f$ is the $\\pi$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in[0,\\pi).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi)=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi$-periodic, because for any $x\\in\\mathbb{R},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi$-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=f(0)=F(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\left(x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\neq f(x)$ for $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535092 true] [17592186166495 1100 "$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) g(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ defined above, compute $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(6x), \\cos(4x) \\rangle$ and determine if functions $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product." 13194139535092 true] [17592186166495 1100 "$\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) g(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x$\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $[MATH: x\\in [0,2\\pi)]$ defined above, compute $\\displaystyle \\langle \\sin(6x), \\cos(4x) \\rangle$ and determine if functions $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ are orthogonal with respect to the inner product." 13194139535092 false] [17592186166495 1145 "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\sin(6x)\\cos(4x)\n=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\sin(10x)+\\sin(2x)\\big).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{4}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\sin(6x)$ and $g(x)=\\cos(4x)$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139535092 true] [17592186166495 1145 "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\sin(6x)\\cos(4x) =\\dfrac{1}{2}\\big(\\sin(10x)+\\sin(2x)\\big)\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨\\sin (6x), \\cos (4x)⟩ &= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\sin (6x)\\cos (4x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (10x) + \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2x)dx \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{1}{20}\\cos (10x) - \\frac{1}{4}\\cos (2x)]_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{20}(\\cos (20π) - \\cos (0)) - \\frac{1}{4}(\\cos (4π) - \\cos (0)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{20}(1 - 1) - \\frac{1}{4}(1 - 1) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sin(6x)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\cos(4x)\n$$\n\n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." 13194139535092 false] [17592186166496 1100 "Let $F(x) = 2x^2+5$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R$, and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $[MATH: x\\in [0,2\\pi)]$ to $x\\in \\mathbb R$. The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n$f(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx)\\right)$.\nDetermine the integral expression for the coefficient $a_4$." 13194139535092 false] [17592186166496 1100 "Let $F(x) = 2x^2+5$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi$-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ to $x\\in \\mathbb R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\left(a_n \\cos(nx) + b_n \\sin(nx)\\right).\n$$\n\nDetermine the integral expression for the coefficient $a_4.$" 13194139535092 true] [17592186166496 1145 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $[MATH: x\\in [0,2\\pi)]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $[MATH: x\\in [0,2\\pi),]$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0$, $a_n$, and $b_n$ are constants to be determined.\n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\cos(4x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(x), \\cos (4x)⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx)), \\cos (4x)⟩ &=  \\\\\n⟨\\frac{a_{0}}{2}, \\cos (4x)⟩ + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)⟨a_{n}\\cos (nx), \\cos (4x)⟩ + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)⟨b_{n}\\sin (nx), \\cos (4x)⟩ &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{0}}{2}⟨1, \\cos (4x)⟩ + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)a_{n}⟨\\cos (nx), \\cos (4x)⟩ + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)b_{n}⟨\\sin (nx), \\cos (4x)⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1, \\sin(x), \\cos(x), \\sin(2x), \\cos(2x), \\ldots$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\na_4 \\left\\langle \\cos(4x), \\cos(4x) \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨\\cos (4x), \\cos (4x)⟩ &= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\cos^{2} (4x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (8x)dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{16}\\sin (8x)]_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2π - 0) - \\frac{1}{16}(\\sin (16π) - \\sin (0)) \\\\\n&= π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨f(x), \\cos (4x)⟩ &= a_{4}⟨\\cos (4x), \\cos (4x)⟩ \\\\\na_{4} &= (⟨f(x), \\cos (4x)⟩)/(⟨\\cos (4x), \\cos (4x)⟩) \\\\\na_{4} &= \\frac{1}{π}\\int_{0}^{2π}f(x)\\cos (4x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{π} \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2π}(2x^{2} + 5)\\cos (4x)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535092 false] [17592186166496 1145 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\langle f(x),g(x) \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x)g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi$-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left( a_n\\cos(nx)+b_n\\sin(nx)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a_0,$ $a_n,$ and $b_n$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\cos(4x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1, \\sin(x), \\cos(x), \\sin(2x), \\cos(2x), \\ldots$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\na_4 \\left\\langle \\cos(4x), \\cos(4x) \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={a}_{4}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(2{x}^{2}+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535092 true] [17592186166894 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330762 13194139535092 true] [17592186166894 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330778 13194139535092 true] [17592186166894 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330903 13194139535092 true] [17592186166894 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330910 13194139535092 true] [17592186166895 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330769 13194139535092 true] [17592186166895 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330785 13194139535092 true] [17592186166895 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330874 13194139535092 true] [17592186166895 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330883 13194139535092 true] [17592186166895 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330893 13194139535092 true] [17592186166896 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330794 13194139535092 true] [17592186166896 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330814 13194139535092 true] [17592186166896 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330834 13194139535092 true] [17592186166896 1020 #edb/ref 17592186330854 13194139535092 true] [17592186167203 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535092 true] [17592186167203 1100 "Let $F(x) = \\sin (2x)$ for $x ∈ R$. Suppose $f(x)$ is the $π$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $[MATH: x ∈ [0, π)]$ to $x ∈ R$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x + π) = f(x)$ for all $x ∈ R$\n2. $f(x) = F(x)$ for all $x ∈ R$\n3. $f(x + \\frac{5π}{2}) = f(x)$ for all $x ∈ R$" 13194139535092 false] [17592186167203 1100 "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" 13194139535092 true] [17592186167203 1145 "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f$ is $\\pi $-periodic, shifting by $2\\pi $ does not change the value of $f,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f((x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\pi )=f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does not always equal $f(x).$ For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=F(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\\ne f(x)$ for $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139535092 true] [17592186167220 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535092 true] [17592186167220 1100 "Let $F(x) = x^{2}$ for $x ∈ R$, and let $f(x)$ be the $2π$-periodic extension of $F$ from $[MATH: x ∈ [- π, π)]$ to $x ∈ R$. The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$f(x)∼\\frac{a_{0}}{2} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(a_{n}\\cos (nx) + b_{n}\\sin (nx))$.\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient $a_{5}$ below, where $⟨, ⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[MATH: [- π, π).]$\n\n$(⟨\\sin (5x), \\sin (5x)⟩)/(⟨f(x), \\cos (5x)⟩)$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}" 13194139535092 false] [17592186167220 1100 "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{5}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535092 true] [17592186167220 1145 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-10\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={a}_{5}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{5} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535092 true] [17592186330762 1017 #edb/ref 17592186330763 13194139535092 true] [17592186330762 1089 "q-338490" 13194139535092 true] [17592186330762 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535092 true] [17592186330762 1100 "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- $f(x)=f(x-2\\pi )$ for all $x\\in R$" 13194139535092 true] [17592186330762 1113 #uuid "238cb61e-c331-5c28-af91-45990df477fe" 13194139535092 true] [17592186330762 1145 "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =f((x-\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =f((x-2\\pi )+\\pi ) \\\\  & =f(x-2\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139535092 true] [17592186330763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330764 13194139535092 true] [17592186330763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330765 13194139535092 true] [17592186330763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330766 13194139535092 true] [17592186330763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330767 13194139535092 true] [17592186330763 1000 #edb/ref 17592186330768 13194139535092 true] [17592186330763 1040 #edb/ref 175921